  "question": {
    "text": "A compressive force, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>F</mi></math> is applied at the two ends of a long thin steel rod. It is heated, simultaneously, such that its temperature increases by <math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#916;</mi><mi>T</mi></math>. The net change in its length is zero. Let <math><mi>l</mi></math> be the length of the rod, A its area of cross-section, <math><mi>Y</mi></math> its Young&#39;s modulus, and <math><mi>&#945;</mi></math> its coefficient of linear expansion. Then, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>F</mi></math> is equal to:",
